  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "Let $n$ be a composite number greater than $4$. Prove that $n$ divides $(n-1)!$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n\\mid(n-1)!$.\n\nFor a non-square, use two distinct factors of $n$; for $n=a^2$, use the distinct factorial factors $a$ and $2a$.",
      "worked": "If $n$ is not a square, write $n=ab$ with $1<a<b<n$. The distinct integers $a$ and $b$ both occur in $(n-1)!$, so their product $ab=n$ divides $(n-1)!$.\n\nIf $n$ is a square, write $n=a^2$. Since $n>4$, we have $a\\ge3$, so $1<a<2a<a^2=n$. Thus the distinct integers $a$ and $2a$ both occur in $(n-1)!$. Their product is $2a^2=2n$, so $n$ divides $(n-1)!$ in this case too.\n\nTherefore, $n\\mid(n-1)!$ for every composite integer $n>4$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
